General Guidelines and Tips
Crocheting in the Round
In these days I use the Magic Ring method to begin working in the round. An alternative is the “Chain 2”
method which you start with two chains and crochet the required number of single crochets in the
second chain from hook. Either method you use, after you have crocheted a few rounds pull the loose
end strongly to close the hole tightly and fasten off.
Inner side Outer side
During the execution of each component check every now and again if the number of stitches is correct.
My patterns are designed with regular schemes which help follow the pattern. If you end the round with
the same set of stitches the round was started, most likely the work is correct.
If you notice there is something wrong undo the rounds that are wrong and restart for the benefit of the
final outcome.
Starting to Work with an Oval
Make a chain per pattern instructions. Starting from second ch from hook, work your crochet stitches in
each top loop across as mentioned in the pattern until you reach the last chain next to the slipknot
before you make the increase (photos 1 and 2). Work the required number of stitches (increase) into the
bottom loop of the last chain, this will avoid the hole where the increase has been made. Turn your work
upside down to work into the bottom loops of the foundation chain as instructed (photos 3 and 4).
top loop slipknot bottom loop
1 bottom loop 2 bottom loop 3 4
Changing Color
Work the last stitch on round as follows: insert hook in last stitch, yarn over and pull through, yarn over
with the new color and pull it through all stitches on hook. Continue on to next round with the new color.
Stuffing with Fiberfill
Stuff your amigurumi firmly so it retains its shape. Be careful not to overstuff it otherwise the stuffing
may stretch the fabric and show through stitches. Shape the pieces with your hands as you fill in so to
smooth them out, distributing the stuffing evenly inside the part.
Stuffing small parts could be difficult and it may help use the back of a pencil to fill in it.
When attaching the head to the body add some additional fiberfill between them before completely
sewing. This way the head does not hang down.
Polyester fiberfill is advised for stuffing as it is non allergenic and it is widely available in craft shops.
2018 ©MarysAmiland
Mary’s Amiland 9
Amber Snail the Ami
Applying Safety Eyes and Nose
You have already inserted the eyes and/or the nose in the amigurumi’s head and you are ready to fix
them in the back.
First thing to do is to warm up the concave part of the washer. I
usually put it close to the cooktop holding it with a pair of pliers
for few seconds.
Next you place the warmed washer on the rod of the eye in the
back part of the head with the dome side facing you. Push it
down helping yourself with the small plastic dishes that come
along with the safety eyes purchase.
Crocheting Two Identical Items
Any time you have to crochet two identical items e.g. legs, arms, ears or
whatever parts of your amigurumi, plan to do it one after the other because
even though you follow the same pattern, use the same hook and yarn their
sizes could vary if you make them on different days.
The two identical items in the photo below were made one in the evening and
the other the morning after. Same pattern, yarn and hook but different size!
Joining All Parts Together
Assembling is the most critical phase of the project and determines the success of the outcome. Pin all
pieces together before assembling your amigurumi, this allows you to gather an overall picture and to
adjust if necessary. Then for sewing each component you may remove the items you are not working on.
Pay attention to how you sew items together, use neat stitches and hidden stitches where possible. It is
preferable to use the tail left from where you fasten off the piece. If that is not possible use a new length
of yarn of the same color yarn from one of the parts you want to sew together.
Joining Seamless Rounds
The following photos show how to fasten off so the join of the end to the beginning of the round is
seamless.
Thread the yarn through a needle, push it
through the next stitch (photo #1). Put the
needle back in the center of the last stitch
and pull gently (photo #2).
