ButterflyLove’s Materials;https://www.lovecrafts.com/en-gb/p/amigurumi-snowball-knitting-pattern-by-butterflylove1;"Abbreviations
K Knit
P Purl
Inc Increase (by knitting into the front and back of the stitch)
Tog Together
St st Stocking stitch (K 1 row, P 1 row)
Instructions
Cast on 8 stitches, leaving a foot long length of yarn for sewing up
later.
Row 1: Inc across the row (16 stitches)
Row 2 and all following even rows: P
Row 3: Inc across the row (32 stitches)
Row 5: (Inc1, K1), repeat to end of row (48 stitches)
Rows 6-24: St st
Row 25: (K1, K2Tog), repeat to end of row (32 stitches)
Row 26: P
Row 27: K2Tog across the row (16 stitches)
Row 28: P
Row 29: K2Tog across the row (8 stitches)
Leave these eight stitches on your needle with a foot long length of
yarn for sewing up.
Using the tapestry needle, thread the loose end of yarn though the
eight stitches on your knitting needle and pull tight to form the top
of your snowball. Next sew the two sides together, pausing halfway
down to attach your two safety eyes and stuff the snowball with
polyfil. Finally, sew the snowball closed.
TIP - If you have problems getting a good finish with your safety
eyes, try padding the back with a small square of wadding between
the back of each safety eye and the snowball. If you cut a slit into the
wadding, rather than a hole, you will find it much easier to position
it in place.
TIP - To position your eyes at the same height, simply push one eye
through (without fixing it in place) and then follow the row across to
where you want the second eye. Not fixing the first eye in place will
make it much easier to see which row you have pushed your first eye
into.
Lastly, cut a short strip of thin ribbon approximately 12 inches (30
cm) long and thread through the top of the snowball using the
tapestry needle. Tie the two ribbon ends together at the base and
three inches from the top and finish by tying the loose ends into a
neat bow.
