Pattern
Body
You will start with beige yarn
R1: 6 sc in MR
R2: 6 inc = 12 sc
R3: sc, inc 6x = 18 sc
R4: 2 sc, inc 6x = 24 sc
R5: 3 sc, inc 6x = 30 sc
R6–R10: 16 sc (beige), 6 sc (brown), 8 sc (beige) = 30 sc
R11: 13 sc, 6 dec, 5 sc = 24 sc
R12–R16: 24 sc
R17: 11 sc, inc 2x = 26 sc
R18: 12 sc, inc 2x = 28 sc
R19: 13 sc, inc 2x = 30 sc
R20–R21: 30 sc
R22: 3 sc, dec 6x = 24
R23: 2 sc, dec 6x = 18
R24: sc, dec 6x = 12
After this round, you can start adding stuffing inside the
capybara. Make sure to distribute it evenly to maintain
the shape.
R25: dec 6x = 6 sc
Fasten off and close the hole. Weave in the yarn end.
R2 R6 R11
R16 R21 R24 < After stuffing
Body - Closing
To finish the body of your capybara, first cut the yarn, leaving a generous tail. Using a
tapestry needle, weave the yarn through the front loops of all the single crochet
stitches from Round 23 - there should be 6 loops in total. Once the yarn is threaded
through all the stitches, insert the needle into the center of the opening and pull tight
to close the hole. Be careful to not pull too tightly, as this can cause the shape to
distort. Pull just enough to close the gap evenly. After that, simply weave in the
remaining tail to secure and hide the yarn.
hide inside the >
capybara
Ears Back Paws
Worked entirely in brown Worked entirely in brown
R1: 6 sc in MR R1: 6 sc in MR
R2-R3: 6 sc
Front Paws
R1: 6 sc in MR (Brown)
R2-R4: 6 sc (Beige)
Finishing
Now that you have finished all the parts,
it’s time to assemble your capybara! Start
by sewing the ears to the top of the head,
positioning them evenly on both sides.
Then, attach the front paws to the sides of
the body and the back paws to the
bottom, making sure they are aligned.
Make secure stitches and hide the ends of
the yarn inside the body for a neat finish.
With everything sewn, finish by cutting a
piece of black yarn to place where the
eyes will go. Adjust the position until it
looks right, then secure it in place.
In case you need it, I left this image as a reference
for how many stitches after the snout you should
place the capybara’s ears and eyes.
For the snout, use black yarn to embroider a Y-shaped
pattern, as shown in the image.
Attaching the keychain
First, insert the needle into the spot shown in the image and pull out a small loop of
beige yarn, this is where you’ll attach the keychain. Once the keychain is in place, tie
it securely with a knot. Trim any excess yarn and hide the knot inside the capybara.
And that’s it! Your capybara is all done and ready
to shine. You’ve crafted something truly special,
and I hope this little creation brings a smile to your
face. Enjoy your work, and be proud of what
you’ve made!";;Lovecrafts
