Copyright © 2017 Irina Ferrer Davydova, ElvesWorld;https://www.lovecrafts.com/en-gb/p/easter-bunny-toy-knitting-pattern-by-elvesworld;"Abbreviations used:
k – knit
p – purl
kfb – increase (knit the same stitch twice: first through the front, then again through the
back loop)
k2tog – knit two stitches together (1 decreased)
RS – right side row
WS – wrong side row
st(s) – stitch(es)
Doll’s head.
Cast on 9 sts using crochet cast-on in the round (see link in the supplement “useful
links”). Distribute sts evenly onto 3 needles, pull the first stitch taut and begin to work
in the round. Take care not to twist the sts. Place a marker at the beginning of the round.
Round 1: kfb (9 times) (18 sts)
Round 2: stockinette stitch (18 sts)
Round 3: (kfb, k5) 3 times (21 sts)
Rounds 4 – 12: 9 rounds stockinette stitch (21 sts)
Round 13: (k2tog, k5) 3 times (18 sts)
Round 14: stockinette stitch (18 sts)
Round 15: (k2tog, k1) 6 times (12 sts)
Round 16: stockinette stitch (12 sts)
Round 17: k2tog 6 times (6 sts)
Round 18: stockinette stitch (6 sts)
Stuff firmly with polyester filling or wool roving.
Break the working yarn, pull through the remaining 6 sts and pull taut. Hide the yarn
tail inside the head, secure and trim end.
Body
The body is worked from the bottom up.
In Turquoise and using crochet cast-on in the round, cast on 6 sts. Distribute sts evenly
onto 3 needles, pull the first stitch taut and start to work in the round.
Take care not to twist the sts. Place a marker at the beginning of the round.
Round 1: kfb 6 times (12 sts)
Round 2: (kfb, k1) 6 times (18 sts)
Round 3: (kfb, k2) 6 times (24 sts)
Round 4: (kfb, k3) 6 times (30 sts)
Round 5: (kfb, k4) 6 times (36 sts)
Round 6: (kfb, k5) 6 times (42 sts)
Round 7: reverse stockinette (42 sts)
Rounds 8 – 16: 9 rounds stockinette stitch (42 sts)
Rounds 17 – 18: 2 rounds reverse stockinette (42 sts)
Change color to Lilac.
Rounds 19 – 24: 6 rounds stockinette stitch (42 sts)
Round 25: (k2tog, k5) 2 times, k2tog, k12, (k2tog, k5) 2 times (37 sts)
Round 26: (k2tog, k4) 2 times, k2tog, k11, (k2tog, k4) 2 times (32 sts)
Round 27: (k2tog, k3) 2 times, k2tog, k10, (k2tog, k3) 2 times (27 sts)
Round 28: stockinette stitch (27 sts)
Round 29: (k2tog, k2) 2 times, k2tog, k9, (k2tog, k2) 2 times (22 sts)
Round 30: (k2tog, k1) 2 times, (k2tog, k3) 2 times, (k2tog, k1) 2 times (16 sts)
Round 31: k4, (k2tog, k2) 2 times, k4 (14 sts)
Bind off 14 sts knit-wise and stuff the body. The upper edge of the body is still open.
Hands (make 2).
Cast on 4 sts. Either work as stated above (as for head or body), or knit i-cord (link to
video in the supplement “useful links”), which is faster and easier for small diameter
tubes as this one.
Rounds 1–12: 12 rounds stockinette stitch (4 sts)
Do not fill.
Break the working yarn, pull through the remaining 4 sts and pull taut. Hide one of the
two ends inside the tube, leave hanging at the other end, to be used for joining with
sleeve later.
Sleeves (make 2)
Worked in the round.
In Lilac and using long-tail cast-on, cast on 8 sts.
Distribute sts evenly onto 3 needles and begin to work in the round. Take care not to
twist the sts. Place a marker at the beginning of the round.
Rounds 1–12: 12 rounds stockinette stitch (8 sts)
Bind off knit-wise.
Ears (make 2)
Worked flat in color White on two needles in back- and forth rows.
Cast on 7 sts.
Rows 1–8: stockinette stitch in rows (7 sts)
Row 9: k2, k2tog, k3 (6 sts)
Row 10: p6 (6 sts)
Row 11: k2, k2tog, k2 (5 sts)
Row 12: p5 (6 sts)
Row 13: k2, k2tog, k1 (4 sts)
Row 14: p4 (4 sts)
Row 15: k2tog 2 times (2 sts)
Row 16: p2tog (1 sts)
Cut yarn. Draw tail through remaining stitch and pull tightly and secure.
Legs (make 2)
In White and using crochet cast-on in the round, cast on 6 sts. Distribute sts evenly onto
3 needles, pull the first stitch taut and start to work in the round.
Take care not to twist the sts. Place a marker at the beginning of the round.
Round 1: (kfb, k1) 3 times (9 sts)
Rounds 2–13: 12 rounds stockinette stitch (9 sts)
Do not fill.
Break the working yarn, pull through the remaining 9 sts and pull taut. Hide one of the
two ends inside the tube, leave hanging at the other end, to be used for joining with
body later.
Embroidering a face.
Embroider the nose and mouth. Photos below illustrate the process.
Assembling the doll.
Fold each ear in half and sew to the head with invisible stitches around.
Photos below illustrate the process.
Eyes
Mark the spots where the eyes will go with pins. Move the pins a little to find the best
spot.
Thread a long needle with sturdy black thread or a piece of mercerized cotton. Insert the
needle into the spot where the head connects with the body, leaving a tail of 15 cm [5.9
in] on the outside. Exit the needle in the marked spot of the doll’s future face. Now,
remove the pin, pull the thread through the opening in the bead or the hook of the sew-
on eye, and, inserting the needle again into the same spot, exit the thread in the
beginning spot. Try to end up in the same spot where you previously left the tail. Pull
the thread taut, to ease the eye in place, now leaving two ends of equal length. Those
will be secured with a knot a little later. Repeat the process for the other eye.
The process shown below on a grey mouse uses the same method.
Now, grasp both thread ends from both eyes together and pull to form the eye sockets,
checking that the eyes are correctly aligned. Knot the strands from each eye with
themselves, adjusting tension as needed. Hide the ends in the filling. Trim any excess.
Sew the head to the body with invisible stitches around.
Pin the legs to the body and sew them.
Insert the arms into the sleeves with the aid of a crochet hook. Sew the top edge of the
sleeve closed, at the same time attaching the sleeve to the arm with a few small stitches.
Hide the end inside the arm.
Sewing on the arms.
First, pin the arms to the body, to determine the best spot.
Let’s look into the process of sewing on the arms with the “thread hinge” method,
shown on the example of the “Pippi” doll.
With thread held double and going right through the body, sew the arms on:
Insert the needle through the body and the first arm, and go back with the needle,
creating an almost invisible stitch. Go with the needle through the body again and attach
the other arm.
This has to be done several times for durability, always inserting the needle into the
same spot, for the limbs to stay moveable. End on the inside between the body and the
arm. Secure the end, and hide the tail within the body.
Photos below illustrate the process.
Suspenders (make 2)
Crochet a chain of 28.
On both sides leave the end of the thread about 10 cm [4 inches] for sewing to the toy.
Sew suspenders and decorative buttons as shown in the photos.
Our doll is ready!
