How to Read the Pattern
This pattern uses American crochet terminology.
Consider the following directions when you are making your amigurumi from this pattern:
- working in the round (abbreviation: Rnd): crochet in a continuous spiral, making note of where the
rounds begin and end by marking the end of a round with a stitch marker. Do not join rounds unless
otherwise instructed.
- working in rows (abbreviation: Row): work in rows, turning the work at the end. Each row ends with a
chain which is not counted in the number of stitches on the hook, that is the number in parentheses at the
end of the row instruction in the pattern. Note that if the chain is not needed it is not listed at the end of
the row in the pattern instructions.
When counts of round and row are sequential, it means that the piece is worked as a continuation from
one to the other without cutting the yarn. For example, “Row 1, Row 2, Rnd 3, Rnd 4” means start the
piece working in rows and continue to work in the round after Row 2.
The Magic Ring method (or Adjustable Ring) is used to start crocheting in the round, feel free to replace it
with “Chain 2” method if you wish.
This pattern uses single crochet invisible decrease abbreviated as dec. Find instructions in “Basic Stitches”
in the appendix. If you do not feel comfortable with this method feel free to make the traditional
decrease.
Find instructions for crochet stitches used in this pattern and conversion charts in the appendix at the end
of the document.
COLOR CODES IN THE PATTERN
 C1 (beige) = dog
 C2 (blue) = collar
 C3 (yellow) = tag
ABBREVIATIONS IN THE PATTERN
ch = chain dec = decrease
st(s) = stitch(es) rep = repeat
sl st = slip stitch Rnd = work in the round
sc = single crochet Row = work in rows
 In [] = stitches to repeat for the indicated number of times, e.g. “[2sc in next st, sc 2] three times”
means repeat “2sc in next st, sc 2” three times;
 Between*…*= stitches to repeat for the entire round;
 In () = stitch count at the end of the round/row.
