Special Crochet Techniques
Crochet in Spiral Rounds:
Work the body, hands, feet, fingers, tail, ears, yellow face, sombrero cone in spiral rounds (do not
turn, do not join). Use a stitch marker or pin to mark the end of each round. Move the marker up
each time you finish a round.
Invisible Fastening Off:
For a smooth finish to a final round, simply break the yarn
leaving an end long enough to sew with. Pull it through the
loop of the last stitch. Thread it into a wool needle and take
the needle under the two strands of the first stitch.
Pic.1
Then take it back into the last stitch to form a new stitch, or
just pull it tight until it disappears.
Pic.2
Changing Colours in Continuous Rounds:
1. At the start of a round which is to be in a new colour, pull
up a loop as if to make a double crochet in the old colour, but
don't pull through the second loop to complete the stitch, you
should then have two loops on your hook.
Complete the double crochet by pulling through a loop the
*new* colour. You have just made a double crochet which is
half the old colour and half the new colour.
Pic.3
2. Continue double crochet with the new colour.
Pic.4
Closing the Hole of Remaining Stitches:
Once you have finished the final round there remains a small
opening which you need to close.
1. Thread the yarn tail into a tapestry needle.
2. Insert the needle through the front loop of each stitch
around the opening (pic.5) and pull the tail tight to close the Pic.5
hole (pic.6).
To get rid of the bump, insert the needle through the middle of the ring you’ve created (pic.7), and
bring it out 4-5cm away. Pull the yarn tight and the bump at the closed end will disappear, leaving
a nice smooth finish.
Finally, snip the end close to the surface, and the cut end will retract back inside the piece (pic.8-
9).
Pic.6 Pic.7 Pic.8 Pic.9
Slip/Magic Ring:
To make a slip ring form a loop with your yarn. You will need to loop the yarn around your
fingers so that the end attached to the yarn ball, lies on the right while the ""tail"" of the yarn lies
on the left. Insert the hook through the loop (under both pieces of yarn). Pull the portion of yarn
through the loop and put your first row of stitches into the ring. Pull the short end of yarn and the
stitches will close together at the centre, completing your slip/magic ring (pic.10-13). To close
this round make a slip stitch into the first stitch of your ring and proceed to the next round, or
don't make a slip stitch and continue to crochet to next stitch if you are working in spiral rounds.
Pic.10 Pic.11 Pic.12 Pic.13
