Ken’s Hat;https://www.lovecrafts.com/en-gb/p/kens-hat-crochet-pattern-by-elise-mann;"abbreviations to translate.
Materials:
1 ‘ball’ of Hoooked ribbon XL in your chosen colour,
9mm crochet hook
Large-eye needle (or smaller hook) to finish the ends (pull through inside).
Size:
Medium (fits me, fairly small woman’s head) about 52cm, 20.5” inside around,
Large (fits my husband, large size man’s head) 56cm, 22” around. If you need
something in between, try a 10mm hook, or add a fabric lining.
Tension:
About 9 dc and 10 rows to 10cm (4”) with the 9mm hook.
Abbreviations:
dc – double crochet, (what the US calls sc)
htr – half treble, yarn round hook, yarn round and pull a loop through, as if you were
doing a treble, but pull the next yarn round through all three loops on the hook to finish
the stitch. (US, hdc)
Method:
Top:
Row 1: 6 dc into a magic ring.
Row 2: 2dc into each dc (12)
Row 3: dc, 2dc in next dc (18)
Row 4: 2 dc, 2dc in next dc (24)
Row 5: 3 dc, 2dc in next dc (30)
Row 6: 4 dc, 2dc in next dc (36)
Row 7: 5 dc, 2dc in next dc (42)
Size 1:
Row 8: 6 htr, 2htr in next dc (48).
The base is now about 17cm, about 7 inches, across the widest point. This is enough for
a basket or a woman's small size hat (fits me, or as a pill-box hat for a larger head).
Size 2:
Row 8: 6 dc, 2dc in next dc (48)
Row 9: 7 htr, 2htr in next dc (54)
This should fit a large head – my husband takes about the largest man’s hat you can
easily buy, and this fits him. I think you can see how to make it bigger if you prefer!
Start the side:
Next Row: dc into back loop AND the extra loop of the htr at the back of the stitch, all
the way around (48 - or 54 for the larger size). This makes the stitch sit upright on the
base, but gives extra strength from the htr loop, and leaves a pretty edge ridge. You
will see it looks a bit like the top of a crochet stitch but at the side, so it’s quite easy to
do once you see it.
Continue with dc in every stitch for 6 rows more, or whatever height pleases you. Just
stop when you like and finish with a slip stitch, pull the yarn through the last loop and
tuck in the end. If you do stop at 6 more, you will have enough yarn from a full ‘ball’ for
another hat, at least a small size one.";;Lovecrafts
