Invisible finish (Inv.FO) and Invisible color change (Inv.CC)
1 2 3 4
First, make an invisible finish (inv.FO) : break free from the yarn then thread it on your
tapestry needle (pic.1). Skip a stitch and insert it in the next one from front to back (pic.2).
Then, insert it in the back loop of the stitch your thread originally came out from (pic.3). Pull
the thread gently until your “fake” stitch has the same size as the others (pic.4). Weave in
the end on the wrong side being careful not to pull the thread more.
[video] How to make an invisible finish
5 6 7 8
Then, introduce the new color : make a slipknot with the new color yarn (pic.5). Insert your
hook through the slipknot and a stitch of your work, just before or after the Inv.FO you just
made (pic.6), then make a regular single crochet (pic.7)- this counts as the first single cro-
chet of the row. You can now carry on with the instructions of the pattern.
[video] How to introduce a new color
Special stitch : the spike stitch (sp.st)
9 10 11
Basically, a spike stitch is an elongated single crochet : rather than inserting your hook in the
next stitch as you would normally do, insert it in one (or more if so stated) row below the one
you’re working on (pic.9), then continue as you normally would with a single crochet (pic.10-
11).
