How to Read the Pattern
This pattern uses American crochet terminology.
Consider these directions when you are making your amigurumi from this pattern:
- working in the round (abbreviation : Rnd): crochet in a continuous spiral, keeping track of where the
rounds begin and end by marking the end of a round with a stitch marker (safety pin). Do not join rounds
unless otherwise noted.
- working in rows (abbreviation: Row): work in rows turning the work at the end. Each row ends with a
chain which is not counted in the number of stitches in brackets. Note that if the chain is not needed it is
not reported at the end of the row.
When counts of round and row are sequential it means that the piece is worked continuing from one to
the other without cutting the yarn. For example “Row 1, Row 2, Rnd 3, Rnd 4” means to start the piece
working in rows and continue to work in the round after Row 2 is done.
The Magic Ring method (or Adjustable Ring) is used to start crocheting in the round, feel free to replace it
with “Chain 2” method if you wish.
This pattern uses single crochet invisible decrease abbreviated as dec. Find instructions in “Basic
Stitches” in the appendix. If you do not feel comfortable with this method feel free to make the
traditional decrease.
Find instructions for crochet stitches used in this pattern and conversion charts in the appendix at the
end of the document.
ABBREVIATIONS IN THE PATTERN
ch = chain BLO = crochet into back loop only
st(s) = stitch(es) FLO = crochet into front loop only
sl st = slip stitch dec = decrease
sc = single crochet rep = repeat
hdc = half double crochet Rnd = work in the round
dc = double crochet Row = work in rows
 In [] = stitches to repeat for the reported numbers, e.g. “[2sc in next st, sc 2] three times” means
repeat “2sc in next st, sc 2” three times;
 Between*…*= stitches to repeat for the entire round;
 In () = stitch count at the end of the round/row.
