Pumpkin;https://www.lovecrafts.com/en-gb/p/pumpkin-crochet-pattern-by-texasstitchchicks;"Abbreviations
bl: back loop
ch: chain
rnd(s): round(s)
sc: single crochet
st(s): stitch(es)
sl st: slip stitch
inc: single crochet 2 in stitch
dec: single crochet 2 together
yo: yarn over
Materials
Size 3.00mm crochet hook
Tapestry needle
Polyester stuffing
Locking stitch markers (for counting rounds, optional)
Yarn
This";"pattern can be used with any weight yarn. I usually go down 2 or
3 crochet hook sizes from what is listed on the yarn band. For a
larger animal, try a bulky weight yarn. Just remember that you will
use more yardage than recommended. The sample is crocheted in DK
weight yarn and all yardage and hook recommendations are calculated
based on the sample.
To complete the pattern, you’ll need:
MC – Orange (Plymouth Yarn, Dreambaby DK in Tomato, #153), 30 yards
A few yards of brown yarn for stem
©2020 TexasStitchChicks. This pattern is not available for commercial release. This means you may not
copy and sell or digitally distribute the pattern. You may, however, sell items you make with the pattern.
Helpful Instructions and Pattern Notes
Gauge
This pattern doesn’t specify a gauge. Just make sure when you crochet
that the stuffing doesn’t show through and the fabric isn’t too loose.
If the stuffing does show through, then you’ll want to use a smaller
hook.
Crochet through back loop
All stitches in this pattern are crocheted through the back loop.
I like to do this because:
- It looks cute
- It’s super easy to count rounds
- It creates a loop on the outside of
the piece that you can use to attach
pieces
Continuous rounds
This pattern is created by crocheting in continuous rounds, so there
is no need to end a round when you complete it. Because of this, it
may be easier to count rounds by adding a locking stitch marker to the
last stitch of the round.
©2020 TexasStitchChicks. This pattern is not available for commercial release. This means you may not
copy and sell or digitally distribute the pattern. You may, however, sell items you make with the pattern.
Sloppy slip knot
I use the sloppy slip knot to start all the pieces. Here’s how to do
it: (a) wrap the yarn around the hook with the tail in the front
(towards the hook end), (b) yarn over, (c) pull up a loop and crochet
as directed. When you have completed the first round, (d) pull on the
tail to close the hole.
a b
c d
As an alternative, you can do a magic ring.
©2020 TexasStitchChicks. This pattern is not available for commercial release. This means you may not
copy and sell or digitally distribute the pattern. You may, however, sell items you make with the pattern.
Fasten off with long tail
When you fasten off, leave extra yarn (6-12 inches). This end can be
used to attach pieces by threading the end of the yarn through a
tapestry needle and stitching the pieces in place.
Pattern
Body
Make 1, in MC
ch 2
Round 1: sc 6 times in 2nd ch from hook (6)
Round 2: [inc] x 6 (12)
Round 3: [inc, sc in next st] x 6 (18)
Round 4: [inc, sc in next 2 st] x 6 (24)
Round 5: [inc, sc in next 3 st] x 6 (30)
Round 6: [inc, sc in next 4 st] x 6 (36)
Round 7-12: sc in each st (36), 6 rounds
Round 13: [dec, sc in next 4 st] x 6 (30)
Round 14: [dec, sc in next 3 st] x 6 (24)
Round 15: [dec, sc in next 2 st] x 6 (18)
Stuff body.
©2020 TexasStitchChicks. This pattern is not available for commercial release. This means you may not
copy and sell or digitally distribute the pattern. You may, however, sell items you make with the pattern.
Round 16: [dec, sc in next st] x 6 (12)
Add a bit more stuffing.
Round 17: [dec] x 6 (6)
Round 18: sl st first and fourth st together.
Fasten off with really long tail (about 24”).
Stem
Make 1, in cc
The stem is crocheted in rows.
Starting with long tail (about 12”), ch 5
Row 1: sl st in 2nd ch from hook, sl st in next 3 ch (4)
Fasten off with long tail.
Assembly
Place long tail of body on tapestry needle. Insert tapestry needle
into the top of body and out the bottom pulling tightly to create
sections.
©2020 TexasStitchChicks. This pattern is not available for commercial release. This means you may not
copy and sell or digitally distribute the pattern. You may, however, sell items you make with the pattern.
Repeat 5 times to create five sections of
body. Tie knot on bottom of body. Pull tail
through to inside of body and cut yarn.
Using long tails, attach stem to top
of pumpkin, tie yarn tails together,
pull tails through to inside of body
and cut yarn.
You have now completed The Pumpkin!
