*Stuff leg
-The end will be an invisible finish used for CLOSED pieces. The steps are broken down
in steps a through c.
Invisible Finish - Closed
(this method is for CLOSED pieces)
Step a: Cut the yarn and pull up on the hook, drawing your yarn out from your last stitch.
Step b: Thread the yarn onto a dull yarn needle and insert the needle through the next 2
FRONT LOOPS ONLY. Pull yarn through. Repeat to complete the round. Pull the yarn tight to
close.
Step c: Weave through the last round to secure the end and pull tight again. Hide the loose end
through the middle of the invisible finish and inside of your finished piece.
Completing Other Leg
E/9-3.5mmHook
CafeLatte(RedHeart)
* While holding the body upside down, look at the side of the body, with the unworked leg
opening facing you. The stitch marker on the left will be the first stitch of round 1.
Create a slip knot on your hook and insert into the stitch. Make sure you are working
from the outside with the hook. Your hook should be facing into the leg hole (making the
other stitch marker your next stitch). Try to keep your stitch tight when going to the other
marker. It’s a tight squeeze and there still may be a gap.
Working in rounds
1 - 8. Sc around (18)
9. (Sc, dec) around (12)
* Stuff leg and finish off with an invisible finish for CLOSED pieces. Refer to the
instructions for the invisible finish of the first leg for the steps and visual reference.
* If there is a gap, you can use a scrap piece of Cafe Latte yarn and loop a couple of
stitches together around the gap. Pull tight to close, tie off yarn and hide the end
through the body.
Completing Arms
E/9-3.5mmHook
CafeLatte(RedHeart)
* Both arms are worked the same way. Working the arms begin very similarly to the
second leg. While holding the body upside down, look at the side of the body, with the
chosen unworked arm opening facing you. The stitch marker on the left will be the first
stitch of round 1. Create a slip knot on your hook and insert into the stitch. Make sure
you are working from the outside with the hook. Your hook should be facing into the arm
hole (making the other stitch marker your next stitch). Try to keep your stitch tight when
going to the other marker. It’s a tight squeeze again, but you can fix any gap in the same
way as the leg gap.
Working in rounds
1. Sc in next 5, dec, sc in next 5 (11)
2. Inc, sc in next 10 (12)
3. Sc around (12)
4. (Sc in next 5, inc) around (14)
5 - 6. Sc around (14)
7. (Sc in next 5, dec) around (12)
* Stuff arm and finish off with an invisible finish for CLOSED pieces. Refer to the
instructions for the invisible finish of the first leg for the steps and visual reference.
Nose Pad
E/9-3.5mmHook
Buff(RedHeart)6yds
Working in rounds
1. Ch 2, 6sc into the 2nd ch from hook (6)
2. Inc around (12)
3. (Sc, inc) around (18)
4. Sc, inc, (sc in next 2, inc) 5 times, sc (24)
5. (Sc in next 3, inc) around (30)
-The end will be an invisible finish used for OPEN pieces. The steps are broken down in
steps a through d.
Invisible Finish - Open
(this method is for OPEN pieces)
*NOTE: These photos may not depict the color or shape of piece that you
are working on. Regardless, these instructions show how to do the
invisible finishes when they are called for, and are here for reference.
Step a: Cut the yarn and pull up on the hook, drawing your yarn out from your last stitch.
Step b: Thread the yarn onto a dull yarn needle. Skip the first stitch and insert the needle under
BOTH LOOPS of the next stitch.
Step c: Insert needle back into the center of the previous stitch from the last round (the stitch
where the loose end was first drawn from). Pass needle through stitch and back bar of the
stitch.
Step d: Pull slowly to form a ‘false stitch’. Tie off end on back of piece. Hide the end through
some stitches on the back of the piece.
*Insert 16mm safety nose into the center of the nose pad.
Ears
E/9-3.5mmHook
CafeLatte(RedHeart)18yds
Make 2
Working in rounds
1. Ch 2, 6sc into the 2nd ch from hook (6)
2. Inc around (12)
3. (Sc, inc) around (18)
4. Sc, inc, (sc in next 2, inc) 5 times, sc (24)
5. (Sc in next 3, inc) around (30)
6. Sc in next 2, inc, (sc in next 4, inc) 5 times, sc in next 2 (36)
7. Sc in next 2, dec, (sc in next 4, dec) 5 times, sc in next 2 (30)
8. (Sc in next 3, dec) around (24)
9. Sc, dec, (sc in next 2, dec) 5 times, sc (18)
*Slip stitch into next stitch and finish off, leaving a long tail for attaching. Flatten the ear
to give it the ear shape.
Tail
E/9-3.5mmHook
CafeLatte(RedHeart)4yds
Working rounds around a chain
1. Ch 4, sc in the 2nd ch from hook, sc in the next chain, 3sc in the last chain
On other side of chain: sc in next 2 chains, 3sc in last chain (10)
2 - 3. Sc around (10)
*Slip stitch into next stitch and finish off, leaving a long tail for attaching.
Assembly of the Bear
Attaching the Nose Pad
- Pin the nose pad into place, centered with the eyes. The nose post will give the pad a
little bit of a bump. You can stuff with fiberfil to make the nose pad push out further if you
wish. Just stuff as you are attaching the nose pad to the face.
- Using a scrap piece of Buff yarn, insert hook through the head and out through a stitch
under the edge of the nose pad. Leave the beginning tail hanging out of the head, as
you will use it to tie off the end.
- Looping around the stitch, insert hook back into same stitch of the head and out through
a stitch right next to the next nose pad edge stitch.
- Insert needle under through the next stitch on the edge of the nose pad.
- Repeat the steps all around the nose pad edge. When you get to the end of the circle,
insert the needle back through the head of the bear and out through the same hole that
the beginning tail is hanging from.
- Tie the 2 ends together and thread both ends on a needle. Pass the needle back
through the body through the same hole both ends are coming from and out somewhere
else on the head. Pull the knot into the body. Cut the ends.
Connecting the Ears
- The ears are attached on the top of the head, even with the shoulders when you are
looking at the bear from the side. The top of the ears will be connected between the 4th
and 5th round on the head. Sew the ears down to the head, using the long tail.
Connecting the Tail
- The tail is connected on the rear end of the bear, centered 3 rounds above the leg gap.
Sew the tail into place using the long tail.
The Teddy Bear is done!! Congratulations!
